    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-122560",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d22726fc-8e6b-5e22-80c8-fbc2aa245217",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten",
      "problem": "Claire has a bag of candies with $12$ assorted flavors. In how many ways can Claire pick $3$ flavors of candies?",
      "worked_solution": "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $12$ flavors, so $n=12.$ We want to select $3$ of those flavors, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of choices is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{12}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{12!}{(12-3)!\\,3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{9!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10\\cdot \\cancel{9!}}{\\cancel{9!}3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,320}{6} \\\\  & =220.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
